1286 & 1659 & 1179 & 1348 & 1420 & 2862 & 1959 \\
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Mathematical Model:

Decision variables:
\[
x_{ij} = 
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if manager } i \text{ is assigned to project } j \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
for all \( i \in M, j \in P \).

Objective:
\[
\min \sum_{i=1}^{7} \sum_{j=1}^{7} c_{ij} x_{ij}
\]
where \( c_{ij} \) are as given in the matrix above.

Subject to:
1. Each manager is assigned to exactly one project:
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{7} x_{ij} = 1 \quad \forall i \in M
\]

2. Each project is assigned to exactly one manager:
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{7} x_{ij} = 1 \quad \forall j \in P
\]

3. Binary constraints:
\[
x_{ij} \in \{0, 1\} \quad \forall i \in M, j \in P
\]

Where:
- \( c_{ij} \) is the cost for Manager \( i \) to complete Project \( j \), as specified in the cost matrix above.
- \( x_{ij} \) are the assignment decision variables.

This model ensures a one-to-one assignment of managers to projects that minimizes the total cost, using the exact cost data from the CSV file.[0m

